This would require great care, because an "Elbereth"
can survive in a bones file from another player's
game. Do you lose the conduct if that bones file is
sucked into _your_ game? Do you lose that conduct if
you step on the engraving? How many turns on that
engraving counts as "use", and how many as "innocent
transit"? It might, after all, be engraved in a
hallway. It isn't fair to count Elbereth-less only
if you don't engrave Elbereth yourself, but happily
spend 100,000 turns camped on one found in a bones
file next to an altar.

> (Yes I do use it - TONS in Pacifist games ... but
> very little in normal games ... I've ascended an
> Illiterate - but I think thats a bit far to go to
> prove that you don't need to Elbereth)

Well, but ascending an illiterate pacifist then
becomes a measure of a whole new level of skillful
playing, so you are not at the end of what you can
do to play challenging games of NetHack even at your
skill level.

> And for Thoth's sake ... get rid of pudding farming

Why on earth? If you don't like it, don't use it,
but leave it there for those to whom it is fun.

That said, I think it would be a reasonable change
to modify the kills counters from 8 bit to 32 bit,
so that patience techniques highlighted themselves
better in the dumplog. Folks can then make their
own calls on the stylishness of a patience game.

More than that, I think the better answer to all the
"rip this out of the game" calls for various
putative abuses is instead to add tracking and
dump-logging for them, so that others can give the
game their own call as to "stylishness" without
depriving those who enjoy the features of those
features. I was much entertained by the recent Art
Willis posting on taking the tournament rogue high
score in part by pudding farming, and note that
doing so apparently excluded him from several other
prizes he might have won with the same invested
time. Others may blanch at the same behavior
descriptions, but I just craved more details: do you
fooproof everything before starting, or what's the
trick for not being lunched by all those puddings?

Similarly, since it is possible also, as I did, to
play a "farming everything" patience game, by
camping on an altar with a scroll of scare monster
and a ring of slow digestion, I think the dumplog
could be usefully extended with a kills count for
each level and branch, again to highlight such
behaviors for stylishness calls.

While I'm at it, I wish the dumplog would
specifically list all items received by wishes,
together with the turn and level/branch where
they were got, and from what source among prayer,
lamp, potion, or wand. There's a vast difference
in kind between wishes as game starters, and
wishes used in ascension runs, IMO.

xanthian.

Granted, if you don't habitually #quit out of games
where you don't start with the requisites, it is
damn-all hard to _repeat_ that particular altar
squatting abuse.

I've probably played a hundred more wizard games
hoping to do that again, and never gathered all the
needed trio, altar, ring, and scroll, IDed and
available at the same time, between T1 and YASD.
That one game counts as the same kind of "lucky
coincidence" as finding a wand of wishing on level
one, but even more rare.




